# 0001 — The inconsistent incomplete inference system

Status: in progress
Started: 2026-11-19
Last brought up to date: 2026-11-23

This is the main story. Every later story is a part of it.

## What we are inventing

A system in which knowledge is written down in conversation with a language
model, as HyperMarkDown cards and documents in natural language, and in which
the same cards carry the numbers that say how sound that knowledge is. What a
card claims, how strongly, what follows from it, and where it disagrees with
another card are all recorded inside the card or computed from it, and each
number leads back to the sentences it came from.

The three words of the name are the three things the system admits about
itself.

- **Inconsistent.** Cards may contradict each other. A contradiction is not
  an error that stops the system. It is a located fact about named claims,
  and the system counts such facts.
- **Incomplete.** Not everything said is formalised, and not everything
  formalised is decided. A claim with no formula stays in natural language. A
  card that is silent on a statement has not denied it.
- **Inference.** From what the cards assert, further statements are derived
  by written rules, and each derived statement carries its derivation.

## Why

A knowledge base written in prose can be read and cannot be measured. Nobody
can say how much of it is contradicted, which part is the unsound one, or
whether last week's edits made it better or worse.

A service in production has this solved. It reports health figures, and one
can say that it behaves well, badly or strangely, and where. We want the same
for knowledge: a figure for the whole base, a figure for each domain, and for
each figure the proofs that stand behind it.

Classical logic cannot give that figure, because in it one contradiction
makes everything provable, and a base is then either consistent or worthless.
A base written by people and models over time is never fully consistent. So
the system must keep working while inconsistent, and say by how much.

## Properties

The system is meant to be three things.

- **Simple.** One shape of formula, `predicate(first, second)`. One place for
  it, the header of the card that says it. Rules short enough to read aloud.
- **Precise.** Every number has an address. A score, a derived statement or a
  contradiction names the claims it rests on as `card#id`. The rules of the
  logic are themselves proved by machine.
- **Customizable.** The predicates, the rules of inference, the schema of the
  graph and the north stars are data in card headers, not code. Another
  knowledge base changes them and keeps the machinery.

And it must do these things.

- **It works with a language model.** The model helps write cards, cut them
  into claims and translate claims into formulas. Whether a formula says what
  a sentence meant is a judgment, so every such step is recorded and can be
  checked by a person or by a second translation.
- **Natural language comes first.** The sentence is the claim. The formula is
  a second writing of it, added only where it fits without distortion.
- **The numbers live in the cards.** A card holds its own claims, credences
  and formulas. Nothing about a card is kept in a database beside it.
- **Proofs are evidence.** A derived statement can show its derivation down
  to the claims, and that derivation is what is offered when the statement is
  questioned.
- **Inconsistency is measured.** For any set of cards the system can say what
  share of its statements is contradicted, and name them.

## So far

**2026-11-19. A vault.** The repository starts as an HMD vault and an HQL
project, with the queries designed ahead of the language where needed.

**2026-11-20 to 2026-11-22. A knowledge base to work on.** The subject is
strange loops: self-reference, level crossing, Gödel's construction, and
whether a set of documents can close such a loop on itself. Cards are added
for logic, computation, reasoning and the cognitive operations. A second
vault holds agent cards. The wiki is split into the knowledge base and a
third vault for designs that do not run yet.

**2026-11-22 to 2026-11-23. Prose becomes countable.** This is the
rudimentary method, described in the
[methodology](../../methodology/index.hmd).

- Each concept card is cut into **claims**, held in its header. A claim has
  an address that is never reused, an evidence grade and a credence.
- Claims that fit are also written as **atoms**. Collected over all cards, an
  atom is asserted, denied, unsaid or contradicted. These are Belnap's four
  values, and they are why a contradiction stays where it is.
- Each link gets a **cross score**: how far its two cards disagree on the
  atoms they share.
- What recurs across cards is stated once as a **north star**. There are
  eight, and they are checked as a cover of the base.
- Headers and links are read as a **typed graph** and checked against a
  schema. The schema is held in a card of the same graph.

**2026-11-23. Atoms become a formal system.**

- A [proposal](../proposals/0001-formal-system-for-claims.hmd) observes that
  the atoms had a language and a valuation and no rules, so nothing could be
  derived and two atoms were compared only when they were the same string.
- The concepts the method had been using without owning get cards:
  `formal-system` and `atom`.
- **Rules of inference** are written as data, in the header of the card that
  holds the schema.
- A script, [derive.py](../../scripts/derive.py), applies the rules until
  nothing new follows. It prints each derived atom with its derivation, and
  each derived atom that some claim denies.
- [From distance to logic](../../methodology/distance-to-logic.hmd) gives the
  logic underneath: a distance with a direction is read as how far one thing
  is from implying another, so that implication is the distance zero and a
  proof is a path whose costs add. The rules are **proved in Lean**.
- The first rule is tried and thrown out. A rule reading `generalizes` as
  `is-a` backwards gave four atoms, and two of them read false, so it was
  removed and the test recorded in
  [derivation](../../methodology/derivation.hmd). This sets the practice: a
  rule stays only if every atom it gives has been read.
- A credence becomes a cost, its surprise in bits, so a derived atom gets a
  belief from the chain it rests on.
- A second script, [claim_distance.py](../../scripts/claim_distance.py),
  measures how close two claim texts are by compression and by shared
  vocabulary. It can propose that two claims say the same thing. It cannot
  propose that one implies the other.

## Where it stands

As run on 2026-11-23. The work of that day is not committed yet.

| Measure | Value |
|---|---|
| Cards that carry claims | 54 |
| Claims on concept cards | 370 |
| Claims that also have an atom | 254 |
| Distinct atoms | 206 |
| Atoms asserted | 122 |
| Atoms denied | 85 |
| Rules of inference | 9 |
| Atoms derived that no claim states | 7 |
| Contradictions between stated atoms | 1 |
| Contradictions reached by derivation | 0 |
| Lean proofs | build, with none left open |

The one contradiction is `same-as(compression, intelligence)`, asserted by
one claim and denied by another. Two of the seven derived atoms rest on it.

Nearly every `same-as` in the base is a denial, and a denial is never a
premise. About a quarter of the atoms therefore cannot start a derivation.

The pipeline runs from a sentence to a derived statement with its
derivation. A low count of contradictions is not yet good news. The rules
have few chains to follow, so the count says more about what the system can
reach than about how consistent the base is.

## Open

- **The health figure.** Nothing computes a share of contradicted statements,
  for the whole base, for a domain, or for one tree of derivations. This is
  the measure the story is for, and it is the next thing to build. A first
  reading by hand is in the
  [consolidation of 2026-11-23](../discussion/2026-11-23/consolidation.hmd).
  It shows that one percentage is not enough: the figure is a small table of
  contradiction, coverage and reach.
- **Coverage beside consistency.** A base with few atoms is trivially
  consistent. The figure has to be reported with the share of claims that
  have no atom, or it flatters.
- **Proofs for the claims graded `proved`.** Lean proves the rules of the
  logic. No mathematical claim of the base is yet tied to a checked proof.
- **Rules that are safe.** A wrong rule produces wrong atoms at scale. Each
  rule has to be kept only if every atom it gives has been read.
- **Contradictions outside the claims.** Thirteen pairs of cards declare a
  one-way relation in both directions. They are in the links, so a figure
  that reads only atoms misses them.
- **Claims with no atom.** A third of the claims are outside the system.
  Comparing them needs a measure that reads meaning.
- **Credences that move.** Every credence is still the prior of its evidence
  grade. Nothing has been updated by a test or a challenge.
- **One language.** Derivation runs in Python because the query language
  cannot yet express a fixed point.
